WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
Let’s talk about the mutations.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.200
The virus that causes COVID-19 keeps changing.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.600
There are a bunch of new versions and they’re more contagious.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.640
One’s hit the UK hard and is spreading around the world. 

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.640
There are others — in South Africa, Brazil, Nigeria too.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.800
But should we be worried?

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.320
So how big a problem are these mutations?

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.240
Will the vaccines still work?

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.600
And the big question many of us want answered  — when will this all end?

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.040
2020 gave us the kind of pandemic no one alive today had ever seen before.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:05.120
A year later 2021 is starting out with the same pandemic — only now it’s on steroids.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.400
One reason is there are new versions of the virus.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.960
But scientists say that’s not exactly a surprise.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.120
Viruses mutate all the time.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.400
It's just what they do.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.920
Viruses usually spread within species — animal to animal, person to person.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.520
But sometimes they mutate and do things like jump the species barrier, say.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:29.640
That’s what happened with swine flu, MERS and this one — SARS-CoV-2.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.240
A virus exists to replicate, evolve and spread.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.560
And it does that by hijacking a cell’s reproductive system

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.200
to make copies of itself so it can spread to other cells and on to other hosts. 

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:48.800
And as it gets copied, over and over again, sometimes there are mistakes. Think of them as typos.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.000
Those changes are called mutations.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.040
But every now and then a mutation comes along that makes the virus better at what it does.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.160
The key is opportunity.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.120
For example, healthy people have immune systems that fight the virus for maybe a couple of weeks.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:20.080
But people who are already really sick with weak immune systems could play host to the virus for much longer. 

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.440
It gives the virus more time to mutate.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.040
That’s what they think happened with these new strains.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.000
In the UK a single variant of the virus known as B.1.1.7 has 23 mutations.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.880
It showed up in September 2020.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:03:01.560
And according to the World Health Organization, they think it could be about 70% more contagious than the original Wuhan strain.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.240
Let’s look at it this way using what’s called the reproduction number.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:18.440
This coronavirus was known to spread from one person to two to three other people on average. 

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.480
The R number was between 2.5 and 3.5. 

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:26.040
That’s without things like social distancing and lockdowns to control the spread.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.680
With those measures the R number came down.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.200
The virus was infecting maybe just one other person or fewer — which is enough to stall the spread.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.120
But then this new UK strain showed up and pushed the R number back up to as high as 1.7.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.520
And this strain has travelled.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.320
But there are other strains too.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.920
It’s important to point out, though, that while these new strains might be easier to catch, 

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:09.560
the WHO says the data coming out of the UK so far suggests no change in disease severity or in the number of people dying.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.920
OK, here’s the next question. Will the vaccines still work on those mutated viruses?

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.160
Well the WHO says right now the answer is yes.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.560
Still, it’s not impossible that more genetic changes down the line mean we need to tweak the vaccines.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.680
It’s why scientists around the world have been keeping an eye on how this virus is changing.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:44.520
They’ve done more than 300,000 gene sequences of the virus since the pandemic began.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.000
So there is a strategy to track and deal with mutations.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.280
But the WHO says that if anything the mutations are a wake-up call.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:59.920
And that it’s up to us — individuals and governments — to do what we can to stop the virus from spreading and mutating in the first place.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.120
Look at what happened at the end of the year.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:09.080
People travelled, they spent time with friends and families

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.160
all against health advice.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.480
And what does all of this mean for the end of the pandemic?

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:40.960
Well, right now the plan is still to vaccinate as many people as possible, to build immunity as fast as possible

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.760
even though vaccines can only do so much.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:02.720
Scientists knew the coronavirus was going to evolve from the get-go.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.920
But the health advice to sit tight and stay safe — that hasn’t changed.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.960
And for those of us who want a hard and fast answer on when this’ll all be over

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:16.600
well, the WHO says anybody who gives you a date — they’d only be guessing.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.400
As with every story about the coronavirus the experts are still trying to work out the science.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.360
And the journalists, well, we’re trying to make sense of it too. And stay as  accurate as possible. 

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:33.640
So make sure to visit trusted sources to get health advice and information

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.160
stay safe and I’ll see you next week. 

